 ## Choosing a talk target
 
 Hold **#** on a DMR channel to open the talk target picker: the channel's default, the reply target, the RX group
-list's talkgroups, recent talkgroups, and "Type a number..." to dial an ID or talkgroup by hand. The **Talk target**
+list's talkgroups, recent talkgroups, and "Dial a number..." to enter an ID or talkgroup. The **Talk target**
 side key opens the same picker.
 
 A talk target has three lifetimes.
 
 | Lifetime | How | Lasts |
 |---|---|---|
-| Once | Focus a talkgroup or person in any list (Contacts, Recent, Find, the picker, actions) and press **PTT** | This call and its reply window |
+| Once | Focus a talkgroup or person in any list (Contacts, Recent, Find, DMR dial, the picker, actions) and press **PTT** | This call and its reply window |
 | For now | Focus it and press **OK**. The radio shows "Talking to ..." | Until you change the channel or press **Back** on Home |
 | Permanent | Focus it and hold **OK**. The radio shows "... is the default now" | Written to the channel |
 
-A permanent talk target must be a contact in the codeplug. A typed number is used for now only. The radio shows
-"Not a stored contact: for now only".
+A permanent talk target must be a contact in the codeplug. The [DMR dial](#typing-a-talkgroup-or-id) sets temporary
+targets, including when you hold **OK** on a saved contact.
 
 ### The talk target picker
 
@@ -71,7 +71,7 @@ Press **Talk target** on a DMR channel (or **\***, **8**). The picker lists, in
 | The last call heard: its talkgroup, or the caller for a private call | reply |
 | The talkgroups of the channel's listen list | listen |
 | Talkgroups from Recent | recent |
-| Type a number... | |
+| Dial a number... | |
 | Select contact... | |
 
 The focus starts on the reply row when there is one.
@@ -83,7 +83,8 @@ The focus starts on the reply row when there is one.
 | Hold **OK** | Makes it the channel's default |
 | **#** | Opens its actions |
 
-"Type a number..." opens Find. Type the talkgroup or ID, focus the line you want and press **PTT** or **OK**.
+Type directly in the picker or select **Dial a number...** first. The dial shows the group call on the first row and
+the private call on the second. A saved contact appears with its name.
 
 "Select contact..." opens [Contacts](#contacts), every contact and talkgroup in the codeplug. Turn the knob, or press
 **Up** and **Down**, to go through them, or type letters of the name or digits of the ID to find one. The keys are the
@@ -95,9 +96,20 @@ On an FM channel **Talk target** shows "Talk target: DMR channels only".
 
 ### Typing a talkgroup or ID
 
-On Home, type the number. Find lists "Talk to TG 3100" and "Private call 3100" (with a name when the ID is a contact).
-Focus one and press **PTT** for one call, or **OK** for now.
+From DMR Home, hold **#** (or press **\***, **8**), then type the number. The group call starts focused.
+Press **Down** to select the private call. Press **PTT** to send one call or **OK** to use the destination for now.
+On a memory channel, **Back** on Home or a channel change restores the programmed target. On the DMR VFO, **OK**
+sets the VFO target. Both rows accept numbers absent from Contacts.
 
+<img src="../ux/screens/160x120i5/dmr_dial.png" width="200" alt="The DMR dial">
+
+On a hotspot or repeater with reflector control by private call, dial the reflector ID and use **PTT** from the
+private row. Use the talkgroup and timeslot documented by your network to speak after linking. For example,
+[openSPOT documents](https://manuals.sharkrf.com/openspot4/en/dmr-usage-notes.html) private calls for linking
+on supporting networks and group calls to TG9 after linking. [BrandMeister](https://news.brandmeister.network/brandmeister-dmr-reflectors-support-ending-by-end-of-2020/)
+retired this reflector function. Dial an ordinary talkgroup there.
+
+Digits typed directly on memory Home still open Find, which includes typed DMR destinations along with other results.
 On a trunked channel, Find also lists "Phone call 5551234" last: **PTT** on it calls that number through the system's
 telephone gateway (the system refuses it when it has none). A call from the telephone network shows the caller's
 number, or "Phone line" when the system didn't send one.
